.Dd 2025-09-30
.Dt X509_CMP_TIME 3ossl
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm X509_cmp_time ,
.Nm X509_cmp_current_time ,
.Nm X509_cmp_timeframe ,
.Nm X509_time_adj ,
.Nm X509_time_adj_ex ,
.Nm X509_gmtime_adj
.Nd X509 time functions
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Ft int
.Fo X509_cmp_time
.Fa "const ASN1_TIME *asn1_time"
.Fa "time_t *in_tm"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo X509_cmp_current_time
.Fa "const ASN1_TIME *asn1_time"
.Fc
.Ft int
.Fo X509_cmp_timeframe
.Fa "const X509_VERIFY_PARAM *vpm"
.Fa "const ASN1_TIME *start"
.Fa "const ASN1_TIME *end"
.Fc
.Ft ASN1_TIME *
.Fo X509_time_adj
.Fa "ASN1_TIME *asn1_time"
.Fa "long offset_sec"
.Fa "time_t *in_tm"
.Fc
.Ft ASN1_TIME *
.Fo X509_time_adj_ex
.Fa "ASN1_TIME *asn1_time"
.Fa "int offset_day"
.Fa "long                              offset_sec"
.Fa "time_t *in_tm"
.Fc
.Ft ASN1_TIME *
.Fo X509_gmtime_adj
.Fa "ASN1_TIME *asn1_time"
.Fa "long offset_sec"
.Fc
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Fn X509_cmp_time
compares the ASN1_TIME in
.Fa asn1_time
with the time in <in_tm>.
.Pp
.Fn X509_cmp_current_time
compares the ASN1_TIME in
.Fa asn1_time
with the current time, expressed as time_t.
.Pp
.Fn X509_cmp_timeframe
compares the given time period with the reference time included in the
verification parameters
.Fa vpm
if they are not NULL and contain
.Sy X509_V_FLAG_USE_CHECK_TIME ;
else the current time is used as reference time.
.Pp
.Fn X509_time_adj_ex
sets the ASN1_TIME structure
.Fa asn1_time
to the time
.Fa offset_day
and
.Fa offset_sec
after
.Fa in_tm .
.Pp
.Fn X509_time_adj
sets the ASN1_TIME structure
.Fa asn1_time
to the time
.Fa offset_sec
after
.Fa in_tm .
This method can only handle second offsets up to the capacity of long,
so the newer
.Fn X509_time_adj_ex
API should be preferred.
.Pp
In both methods, if
.Fa asn1_time
is NULL, a new ASN1_TIME structure is allocated and returned.
.Pp
In all methods, if
.Fa in_tm
is NULL, the current time, expressed as time_t, is used.
.Pp
.Fa asn1_time
must satisfy the ASN1_TIME format mandated by RFC 5280, i.e., its format
must be either YYMMDDHHMMSSZ or YYYYMMDDHHMMSSZ.
.Pp
.Fn X509_gmtime_adj
sets the ASN1_TIME structure
.Fa asn1_time
to the time
.Fa offset_sec
after the current time.
It is equivalent to calling
.Fn X509_time_adj
with the last parameter as NULL.
.Sh BUGS
Unlike many standard comparison functions,
.Fn X509_cmp_time
and
.Fn X509_cmp_current_time
return 0 on error.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn X509_cmp_time
and
.Fn X509_cmp_current_time
return -1 if
.Fa asn1_time
is earlier than, or equal to,
.Fa in_tm
(resp.
current time), and 1 otherwise.
These methods return 0 on error.
.Pp
.Fn X509_cmp_timeframe
returns 0 if
.Fa vpm
is not NULL and the verification parameters do not contain
.Sy X509_V_FLAG_USE_CHECK_TIME
but do contain
.Sy X509_V_FLAG_NO_CHECK_TIME .
Otherwise it returns 1 if the end time is not NULL and the reference
time (which has determined as stated above) is past the end time, -1 if
the start time is not NULL and the reference time is before, else 0 to
indicate that the reference time is in range (implying that the end time
is not before the start time if both are present).
.Pp
.Fn X509_time_adj ,
.Fn X509_time_adj_ex
and
.Fn X509_gmtime_adj
return a pointer to the updated ASN1_TIME structure, and NULL on error.
.Sh HISTORY
.Fn X509_cmp_timeframe
was added in OpenSSL 3.0.
